---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const repoBase = 'https://github.com/NO-BALL-GAMES/NO-BALL-GAMES/blob/main/research';
const truthFiles = [
  {
    title: 'NO BALL GAMES truth baseline',
    label: 'Brand truth',
    href: `${repoBase}/truth.md`,
    summary: 'Defines the safe public position for the sign-flip brand, plus where NFT, WAX and founder claims still need proof.',
    status: 'VERIFIED site position + source-needed token claims',
  },
  {
    title: 'Charlie Buster / Grime Art truth baseline',
    label: 'Artist truth',
    href: `${repoBase}/grimeartist-truth.md`,
    summary: 'Frames Charlie Buster and Grime Art as the visual backbone while flagging founder-history and portfolio claims that still need archive support.',
    status: 'Internal canon + careful public wording',
  },
  {
    title: 'XRP NO BALL GAMES truth baseline',
    label: 'XRPL truth',
    href: `${repoBase}/xrp-noballgames-truth.md`,
    summary: 'Locks the XRP KIDs, Alpha Aaron, FRET, $PMSL and Block Buster OG canon while insisting that issuer, token and marketplace details must be verified.',
    status: 'Internal canon + verify all on-chain specifics',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="NO BALL GAMES Research Vault | Truth Archive, Source Rules & Canon Files"
  description="Research vault for NO BALL GAMES truth baselines, Charlie Buster / Grime Art notes, XRP KIDs canon, rights guidance and source-control references."
  current="research"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">Research vault</span>
    <h1>Archive the proof before building the myth.</h1>
    <p>
      This section is the visible index for the truth archive: the NO BALL GAMES baseline, the Charlie Buster / Grime Art baseline and the XRP NO BALL GAMES baseline. Public pages stay concise because the long-form research lives here first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Truth archive files</h2>
      <p>Read the source markdown before writing public-facing claims. Each file lives in <code>research/</code> and is versioned with the repo.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-grid">
      {truthFiles.map((file) => (
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="kicker">{file.label}</span>
          <h3>{file.title}</h3>
          <p>{file.summary}</p>
          <div class="coming-soon-tag">{file.status}</div>
          <a class="inline-link" href={file.href}>Open markdown source →</a>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section split-grid">
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">How to read the archive</span>
      <h2>Verified, internal canon, source needed.</h2>
      <p>
        The research files separate live public facts from founder-supplied canon and open proof gaps. That keeps the site from turning lore into hard history or chain speculation into technical certainty.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-clean">
        <li><strong>Verified:</strong> safe public facts backed by the repo, site source or other accessible evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Internal canon:</strong> accepted story or brand truth inside the NO BALL GAMES universe.</li>
        <li><strong>Source needed:</strong> usable directionally, but not ready to publish as hard fact without proof.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">Related pages</span>
      <ul class="list-clean">
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}about/`}>About — the sign-flip brand story</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}projects/`}>Projects — where art, lore and XRPL concepts live</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}xrp/`}>XRP page — the concise public summary of the XRPL branch</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}contact/`}>Contact — collector, research and collaboration enquiries</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Why the archive matters</h2>
      <p>Cultural and historical context behind the NO BALL GAMES identity.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">Brand context</span>
        <h3>The sign-flip stays grounded</h3>
        <p>The NO BALL GAMES baseline keeps the public story focused on restricted-space rebellion, street culture and collector-ready brand structure without overclaiming token history.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">Artist context</span>
        <h3>Grime Art needs careful wording</h3>
        <p>The Charlie Buster file gives strong public phrasing for the artist layer while warning where portfolio, role and origin-story claims still need proof.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">XRPL context</span>
        <h3>Lore is not ledger proof</h3>
        <p>The XRP truth file makes the distinction explicit: the 100 / 89 / 11 canon can appear on the site as lore, but issuer and marketplace details must be verified separately.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section split-grid">
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">For the record</span>
      <h2>The public site should stay readable.</h2>
      <p>
        These markdown files are the long-form source material. Public pages should use them for direction, not dump them whole. The result is a cleaner site with clearer proof handling.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">Also in research</span>
      <ul class="list-clean">
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${repoBase}/content-rights.md`}>Content rights note →</a></li>
        <li>Future archive folders can hold imported Substack posts, screenshots and proof logs.</li>
        <li>Use the truth files before adding new lore, token or founder claims.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
